qa(None, "Is your model overfitting? How do you know, and what does it change?",
   f"I compared the R2 on the training women with the R2 on the held-out woman for ridge, random forest and gradient boosting (set D, pooled, {npw}). For DRSP the training R2 was {val(OF, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='ridge'), 'train_R2_mean', '{:.3f}', label='OF train R2 drsp ridge')} for ridge, {val(OF, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='rf'), 'train_R2_mean', '{:.3f}', label='OF train R2 drsp rf')} for the random forest and {val(OF, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='gb'), 'train_R2_mean', '{:.3f}', label='OF train R2 drsp gb')} for boosting, against test R2 of {val(OF, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='ridge'), 'test_R2', '{:.3f}', label='OF test R2 ridge drsp')}, {val(OF, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='rf'), 'test_R2', '{:.3f}', label='OF test R2 rf drsp')} and {val(OF, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='gb'), 'test_R2', '{:.3f}', label='OF test R2 gb drsp')}. The gap (train minus test) was {ci(OF, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='ridge'), 'gap', '{:+.3f}', lo='gap_lo', hi='gap_hi', label='OF gap drsp ridge')} for ridge (interval includes 0) but {ci(OF, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='rf'), 'gap', '{:+.3f}', lo='gap_lo', hi='gap_hi', label='OF gap drsp rf')} for the forest: the flexible models memorise the training women. It changes how to read results: training scores must never be reported; only leave-one-woman-out test scores are, and nested CV (limits to flexibility, chosen without the test woman) keeps the test score of the flexible models from falling below ridge. Learning curves (E5) show the same pattern: a large train-test gap with few women that closes as women are added.")
